  "annotations": [
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "The player chose to mulligan a very keepable 7-card hand. The hand has 5 lands (4 Swamps + Thriving Moor), Devouring Swarm (a solid 3-drop), and Skullslither Worm (a strong 4-drop). While the hand is land-heavy, it has a clean curve of turn 3 Devouring Swarm into turn 4 Skullslither Worm (which forces a discard). The Thriving Moor can even choose green if needed for later splashes. Going to 6 cards means losing a card for no good reason — this hand functions perfectly well and has two quality spells on curve. Land-heavy hands are much better than land-light hands, and mulliganing to 6 in limited is a significant cost.",
      "action_taken": "Mulliganed a 7-card hand with 5 lands, Devouring Swarm, and Skullslither Worm.",
      "better_line": "Keep the 7-card hand. Play Devouring Swarm on turn 3, Skullslither Worm on turn 4, and draw into more spells with the extra card.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 2,
      "player": "MiniMx Minimal",
      "decision_index": 2,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Put the only land in hand (Thriving Moor) on the bottom of the library, leaving a 5-card hand with zero lands. All spells in hand cost 2+ mana, with most costing 3-5 mana. With no lands, the player cannot cast anything and must draw multiple lands before becoming functional, which is devastating after already mulliganing to 5.",
      "action_taken": "Put Thriving Moor on the bottom, keeping a zero-land hand of all spells.",
      "better_line": "Bottom Rise Again (expensive, situational 5-mana sorcery that needs a creature in graveyard) or Grotesque Mutation (reactive combat trick) instead, keeping the only land to have a chance at curving out.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 3,
      "player": "MiniMx Minimal",
      "decision_index": 3,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Bottomed a Swamp instead of Massacre Wurm, leaving only 2 lands in a 5-card hand. While Massacre Wurm is powerful, at 6 mana (3BBB) it's extremely expensive and the player needs to draw multiple lands in a row just to cast their 3-drop on time. A 2-land, 5-card hand is very risky in Limited.",
      "action_taken": "Bottomed a Swamp, keeping a 2-land hand with Massacre Wurm (6 mana)",
      "better_line": "Bottom Massacre Wurm to keep 3 Swamps, ensuring on-curve Liliana's Devotee on turn 3 and Strangling Spores on turn 4. Massacre Wurm is too expensive to justify keeping in a mana-light mulligan hand.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 6,
      "player": "MiniMx Minimal",
      "decision_index": 6,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Playing Forest instead of Thriving Heath on turn 1 when there's no 1-mana play available. The tapped land should be played when the tempo cost is free (turn 1 with no 1-drop). Both lines work for the curve, but playing Heath now choosing green would give flexible mana while 'wasting' the enters-tapped downside on a turn where no mana is needed.",
      "action_taken": "Played Forest turn 1",
      "better_line": "Play Thriving Heath (choosing green) turn 1, taking advantage of having no 1-drop to absorb the enters-tapped tempo loss for free",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 12,
      "player": "Grok4F Humble",
      "decision_index": 9,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Playing Plains instead of Thriving Heath on a turn where neither enables a cast. Thriving Heath enters tapped, making this the ideal turn to play it (no spells castable with 3 mana anyway). Saving Plains for a later turn when entering untapped matters is better sequencing. Additionally, Thriving Heath (choosing green) provides more color flexibility than Plains.",
      "action_taken": "Played Plains (enters untapped but can't use the mana productively this turn)",
      "better_line": "Play Thriving Heath (choosing green) now while you can't cast anything anyway, saving Plains for a turn where entering untapped enables an on-curve play",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 48,
      "player": "Grok4F Humble",
      "decision_index": 20,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Failed to attack with 2/2 Ironshell Beetle when the opponent has no untapped creatures to block (Black Cat is summoning sick) and no untapped lands to cast tricks. This is 2 free damage with zero risk.",
      "action_taken": "Timed out and did not declare any attackers.",
      "better_line": "Attack with Ironshell Beetle for 2 unblocked damage.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 51,
      "player": "Grok4F Humble",
      "decision_index": 22,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Thriving Heath entered the battlefield and the player timed out on the color choice, likely defaulting to Blue instead of choosing Green. With a G/W deck and multiple green cards in hand (Moldgraf Millipede, Nantuko Cultivator, Thriving Grove), choosing Green would have given the Heath flexibility to produce either W or G, smoothing mana significantly. Blue is useless for this deck.",
      "action_taken": "Timed out on Thriving Heath color choice, defaulting to a likely useless color (Blue)",
      "better_line": "Choose Green for Thriving Heath to maximize mana flexibility for the G/W deck, enabling easier casting of green-heavy spells like Moldgraf Millipede and Nantuko Cultivator",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 78,
      "player": "Grok4F Humble",
      "decision_index": 26,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Failed to attack with Ironshell Beetle 2/2 when the opponent had no available blockers (Liliana's Devotee is summoning sick). This was 2 free, uncontested damage that was missed due to timeout.",
